ernments or NGOs who work on this  problem know the way they try to address  it is by doing either free or subsidized  food distribution or food for work  programs trying to create some  employment in that area now those  mechanisms are quite expensive for poor  countries right and also there's some  structural reasons like jobs don't exist  in that area so my idea was instead of  trying to force job creation where there  the economy doesn't support it why not  move people to where the jobs are the  perhaps moving people is going to be  cheaper than moving jobs so so I  followed up on that by conducting a  randomized control trial where you know  I use treatment groups versus control  groups to offer certain kind of program  to people and then track what happened  and in this case I encourage people to  move so in some villages I offered them  the equivalent of about eleven twelve  dollars which paid for a round-trip bus  fare plus a few days of food in other  villages I offered them information on  where they could go right and then I had  a control group where I collected data  but did not have the same thing and then  I tracked what what when what happened  next okay so it turns out the three  bottom-line results are first if you  provide people with some money either  the farm grant or a loan or the travel  cost you can induce people to go so  about a quarter of households try this  out they send a seasonal migrant right  for the period that that's the lean  season at home and when they go their  families consumed six to seven hundred  calories per person per day more and so  they move from two meals a day to three  meals a day which is a remarkably large  effect and the third thing that happened  is a year later and three years later I  continued tracking people in these areas  but I did not repeat the program and a  lot of the people who were induced to  move back in 2008 chose to go back again  in 2009 and 2011 and and then why were  they doing it it turns ou