nking about each of  these problems and studying them  carefully before we launch a big program  to scale this scale there's the idea up  and so that's exactly what we did and so  I'll give you a quick flavor of how this  how this works so imagine you know to in  order to understand the effect on the  rural area so we move from a sample of  3,000 to an area with 30,000 people okay  so we scaled up by 10 times and in order  to study the effect on the rural area  what I did was in some villages I induce  lots of people to move in fact up to 70  percent of people and in some villages I  induce only a few people so it turns out  to be about 35 38 percent okay now with  that variation you can now see if lots  of people move out what happens to the  rest of the people remaining in the  village and that else next answer those  questions about what the spillover  effects are in the village and what you  find is actually even more good news  right really and the good news is when  lots of people move out then there's  less competition for the scarce jobs at  home right so you see that wages rise  because now employers need to pay more  in order to get workers and the people  remaining at home they they have an  easier time finding employment so  they're not as hungry there their income  goes up okay but what happens when  everyone comes back I'm typically how  long I we didn't talk about how long  you're away for right so it's typically  that period the pre-harvest main season  period is about a three month period and  typically people go for a few weeks and  in  they come back after say three or four  weeks because they want to bring money  back home and they want to see the  family and then they go again a second  time so I actually in that migrant  sample I about one point nine two trips  for a family during that period and so  when they come back so then we tracked  you know effects on husband wife's  relationships really attractive like  these kind of sociological effec