e  eastern island  like just not of Australia those are  very poor places where the same  phenomenon of seasonal hunger exists so  we've been working there for the last  few years in partnership with the  government there the planning ministry  and we are now launching the project in  the Indonesian part of Timor like a  province called entity and then just two  weeks ago I was in Sulawesi at another  island Indonesia with a group of Yale  students and we were exploring the  possibilities there okay interesting so  we'll look forward to seeing what goes  on there - final question you know in  reading the Financial Times you know I  originally saw the coverage of your work  there and it describes it as an example  for house for how intelligent  international development and full  philanthropy sille Anthropy should be  done so my question to you are what are  the lessons that we can draw from this  for other research and philanthropic  projects so I think one important lesson  is that we shouldn't take a result even  if it looks very promising and  immediately start applying it elsewhere  right each place is different sure and  the one-size-fits-all policy might not  work so for example when I went to  Indonesia I learned that in Timor even  though people are hungry before the  harvest over there people actually have  land unlike in Bangladesh where most  people are landless right but the land  quality is low so because they have land  they need to stay back home between  planting and harvest to do the little  bit of tasks like weeding so they don't  want to migrate then even though that's  when they're hungry right and so what we  learn is that they're actually willing  to mark migrate after the harvest when  they're free they're more free time  thinking ahead to the fact that they  will be hungry in the next we have is  even right so the program design changed  okay and then we also explored a  replication in Malawi and Zambia so I  actually sent you know we did some  exploration sent a Yale student there to  spend the summer right and there we  learned that in Malawi it turns out that  there is no city with a vibrant labor  market that can absorb migrants sure  right and so we just stopped our  activities there right away so I think  you know thinking about the local  conditions right and then also thinking  carefully about okay if I do a study  with 3000 households how will the  results be  matically different if we do the program  with 30,000 or 300,000 also that's  really important and those are the types  of questions that the government should  be asking right we do research because  we want to inform large-scale policy not  to just start with the research right  but now I think we need to start  developing the science behind scaling up  interventions right so if you take a  small-scale research result is there can  we immediately apply it to large scale  or what are the set of systemic problems  that we will face and how do we address  those questions right because so much  money I think especially previously  mining is thrown at at a problem and you  know there's no real understanding of  what was going on or any follow up so I  think what you're doing is very  important  thank you yes and so the data are  important but this last thing that I  said about systemically thinking about  the science suggests that we also need  to go back to economic theory right and  talk to not just people who analyze data  that people who think about the theory  of change exactly so thank you so much  for being here with us today and sharing  your work thank you very much for more  information about professor mubarak and  his work please visit our website at  macmillan report yale.edu be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Eddie  Macmillan Centre for International and  area studies at you  [Music]
 

